# PEPSE — Peaceful Endless Pandora Side-scrolling Environment

---

## What This Package Does

The `pepse.world.avatar` package handles everything to do with the player
character: movement, physics, keyboard input, animations, and energy. Instead
of putting all of that into one big class, we split it into six classes that
each have a clear job.

- `Avatar` — the main player object; owns physics, animations, and the state
- `AvatarState` — an interface that all three movement states implement
- `IdleState` — behaviour when the avatar stands still
- `RunState` — behaviour when the avatar runs left or right
- `JumpState` — behaviour when the avatar is airborne
- `Energy` — tracks the energy value and notifies the UI when it changes

---

## The State Pattern

The original `Avatar.update()` was turning into a huge if/else chain trying
to figure out whether the avatar was on the ground, moving, jumping,
double-jumping, and so on. It was hard to read and every new feature made
it worse.

To fix this, we created an `AvatarState` interface with two methods:

- `update(Avatar, float deltaTime)` — runs the state's logic, returns next state
- `getAnimation(Avatar)` — returns the right animation for this state

Each movement mode is its own class. The whole `Avatar.update()` loop is now:

    currentState = currentState.update(this, deltaTime);
    renderer().setRenderable(currentState.getAnimation(this));

No if/else chains in `Avatar` at all. Each state handles its own logic and
decides on its own when to switch to a different state.

**`IdleState`** regenerates energy while the avatar is stationary. Every
frame it checks in order: should I jump (space pressed, enough energy)?
Did I walk off a ledge? Is a direction key held with enough energy?
If none of those, it stops horizontal movement and stays idle.

**`RunState`** drains energy per frame, sets the horizontal velocity, and
flips the sprite to face the direction of travel. It also handles the case
where both direction keys are pressed at once (treated as no input). It
switches to `JumpState` on a spacebar press or if the avatar walks off a
ledge, and back to `IdleState` if there is no input or energy runs out.

**`JumpState`** handles all airborne movement. The `doubleJumpUsed` flag
lives inside `JumpState` itself, not in `Avatar`. So when the state object
gets thrown away on landing, the flag resets naturally with it. The double
jump only fires when all four things are true at the same time: the avatar
is falling downward, space was just pressed this frame, the flag is false,
and energy is at least 50.

Adding a new movement mode in the future just means writing one new class
that implements `AvatarState`. Nothing in `Avatar` needs to change.

---

## Energy and the UI Callback

We put the energy logic in its own class to keep `Avatar` from getting too
cluttered. `Energy` stores the value as a float between 0 and 100, clamps
it on every change, and calls a `Consumer<Float>` callback whenever the
value actually changes.

`PepseGameManager` registers the callback like this:

    avatar.setOnEnergyChange(v -> energyLabel.setString(v.intValue() + "%"));

After that, the energy display updates itself automatically. `Energy` doesn't
know anything about the UI — it just calls its listener with the new value.
The callback also fires immediately when it is first registered, so the
display shows "100%" from the start without any extra setup call.

The only energy-related methods that are public on `Avatar` are:

- `addEnergy(float amount)` — called by `Fruit` when the player picks one up
- `setOnEnergyChange(Consumer<Float>)` — used by `PepseGameManager` for the UI

Everything else goes through the internal `Energy` object directly.

---

## Other Implementation Decisions

### Who Can Move the Avatar

`Avatar` has a lot of methods the state classes need — things like
`setVelocityX()`, `isMovingLeft()`, `flipRenderer()`, and `getEnergy()`.
We left all of these without an access modifier, which in Java means
package-private. Only code in the same package (`pepse.world.avatar`) can
call them.

This means the state classes can control the avatar however they need to,
but nothing outside the avatar package can touch those controls. Classes like
`PepseGameManager`, `Terrain`, or `Fruit` only see the two public methods
above and the `PLAYER_SIZE` constant.

### Ground Contact — int Instead of boolean

Using a boolean `isOnGround` flag breaks when the avatar stands on two ground
tiles at once. As it crosses a tile boundary, the exit event for the first
tile fires before the enter event for the second — so the flag briefly goes
false and the avatar wrongly thinks it is in the air for one frame.

We use an `int floorContacts` instead. It goes up on every ground or trunk
collision enter and down on every exit. The avatar counts as grounded as long
as `floorContacts > 0`. We also clamp it to zero in case events arrive in an
unexpected order.

### Jump Input — One Press, One Jump

`isKeyPressed()` returns true for every frame the key is held down. Using it
directly for jumps would fire a jump on every frame while holding space. We
fix this by tracking `wasSpacePressed` (was space held last frame) and
computing `jumpJustPressed` once at the top of each `Avatar.update()` call.
The state classes read it through `isJumpPressed()`. No matter how many times
a state calls it in one frame, the result is the same and nothing breaks.
